            <ArticleTitle>Goal conflict and goal facilitation in community-based cardiac rehabilitation: a theory-based interview study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>227-38</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/13548506.2014.914235</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Theories often consider behaviors in isolation of conflicting and facilitating personal goals. We conducted interviews with 13 people in cardiac rehabilitation, investigating whether eliciting physical activity (PA) control beliefs sufficiently captures goal conflict and goal facilitation. We assessed PA, intention, and control beliefs using standard elicitation methods and then assessed goal conflict and goal facilitation. Twelve participants described conflicting, and all described facilitating, personal goals. Most goal facilitation (94%) and conflict (82%) beliefs were identified beyond the control belief elicitation. Goal facilitation and conflict are not captured in a standard control belief elicitation and may supplement single-behavior models to understand PA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
